\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The two mechanisms on a toy network: two dense clusters (each a $K_4$) joined by two sparse bridges. (a,b)~Preprocessing: the chain tensors on the bridges are rank-non-increasing, so the exact simplification pass absorbs them ($L_2, L_4 \to L_2^*, L_4^*$) before any search begins. (c,d)~Surgery: a converged annealed tree can freeze with a poor waist---the horizontal bipartition (c), which cuts through both clusters---while the cheap comparable-balance cut through the two bridges (d) is invisible to local tree moves; bounded FM on the tensor graph finds it, and both sides are rebuilt cold and spliced.}}{7}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:k4}{{2}{7}{The two mechanisms on a toy network: two dense clusters (each a $K_4$) joined by two sparse bridges. (a,b)~Preprocessing: the chain tensors on the bridges are rank-non-increasing, so the exact simplification pass absorbs them ($L_2, L_4 \to L_2^*, L_4^*$) before any search begins. (c,d)~Surgery: a converged annealed tree can freeze with a poor waist---the horizontal bipartition (c), which cuts through both clusters---while the cheap comparable-balance cut through the two bridges (d) is invisible to local tree moves; bounded FM on the tensor graph finds it, and both sides are rebuilt cold and spliced}{figure.caption.7}{}}
